# How did oil paint make light glow? Van Eyck's Arnolfini Portrait
tags: ["oil paint techniques","renaissance art","light in painting","van eyck","glazing technique","color theory"]
teaches: ["art-history/northern-renaissance","techniques/oil-paint-glazing","concepts/light-reflection","methods/observing-paintings"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, knowledgeable gallery guide, quietly amused","b":"gemini:Puck | a relaxed, good-humoured visitor with a dry, understated wit; curious, never exaggerated"}
prompt: "Where does the light come from in this room? What could oil glazes do that earlier paint couldn't?"
next: {"title":"Can one beam tell a story? Caravaggio's Calling of Saint Matthew","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/how-painters-caught-light/02-can-one-beam-tell-a-story-caravaggio-s.cast"}

## The Arnolfini Portrait: light inside the paint
sources:
    - id: ng
      title: The Arnolfini Portrait (NG186)
      authors: The National Gallery, London
      year: 2024
      finding: Oil on oak, 1434, 82 × 60 cm; the chandelier reflects the light from the window; one lit candle; the signature 'Jan van Eyck was here 1434'; two men in the mirror
      url: https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/jan-van-eyck-the-arnolfini-portrait
    - id: wiki
      title: Arnolfini Portrait
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Thin translucent glazes; light from the window on the left reflected by surfaces; highlights on each bead; oldest famous panels in oil rather than tempera
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnolfini_Portrait
    - id: tempera
      title: Tempera
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Tempera dries rapidly and is laid in small hatched strokes; oil replaced it in the Netherlands in the 15th century
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tempera
    - id: glaze
      title: Glaze (painting technique)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Light travels through a glaze and is reflected back off the layer below
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaze_(painting_technique)
    - id: jve
      title: Jan van Eyck
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Vasari's claim that van Eyck invented oil painting is an oversimplification
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jan_van_Eyck

    card title "The Arnolfini Portrait"

A: How do you put light on a flat surface?
    image art cites ["ng","wiki"] credit "Jan van Eyck, 1434. National Gallery, London. Public domain." x 275 y 330 width 470 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Van_Eyck_-_Arnolfini_Portrait.jpg/1920px-Van_Eyck_-_Arnolfini_Portrait.jpg look screen regions.beads [0.39,0.27,0.03,0.07] regions.candle [0.415,0.05,0.02,0.034] regions.chandelier [0.37,0,0.286,0.198] regions.chest_oranges [0.071,0.522,0.046,0.023] regions.folds [0.56,0.6,0.4,0.37] regions.gown [0.565,0.29,0.405,0.69] regions.man [0.068,0.11,0.443,0.796] regions.man_face [0.25,0.17,0.08,0.09] regions.mirror [0.425,0.241,0.16,0.117] regions.mirror_glass [0.458,0.262,0.09,0.07] regions.mirror_window [0.462,0.276,0.03,0.036] regions.signature [0.417,0.194,0.188,0.038] regions.sill_orange [0.083,0.424,0.05,0.042] regions.window [0.04,0,0.095,0.48] regions.woman [0.536,0.171,0.453,0.812] regions.woman_face [0.705,0.205,0.075,0.085]

A: Over ten paintings, that's our question, and we start in Bruges, in 1434.
    highlight art:window strength soft

B: With a rather well-dressed couple, by the look of it.
    highlight art:man art:woman strength soft

A: This is Jan van Eyck's Arnolfini Portrait: probably an Italian merchant, Giovanni Arnolfini, and his wife.
    portrait vaneyck x 760 y 360 width 220 of "Jan van Eyck" cameo true

    erase vaneyck

A: Van Eyck even signed the wall above the mirror, in Latin: Jan van Eyck was here, 1434.
    highlight art:signature effect detail

B: Very elegant graffiti.
    highlight art:signature effect detail

    camera reset true

B: So where is the light coming from? The chandelier, I'd guess.
    point at.ref art:chandelier gesture glow

A: It holds just one lit candle, in broad daylight. Far too little to light a room.
    highlight art:candle effect detail

    camera reset true

    ask "Where does the daylight come in? Tap the spot." spot window on art voice a
        right: Yes: the open window on the left.
        wrong: Look for the brightest edge of the room.

A: The daylight comes in from the window on the left,
    highlight art:window strength strong lift true

A: and falls across both their faces.
    highlight art:man_face art:woman_face

B: And it doesn't stop at faces. The or (@highlight art:sill_orange effect detail@)ange on the sill has caught it too.

A: So have the brass chandelier and every bead b (@highlight art:chandelier art:beads effect detail@)eside the mirror, each with its own glint of the window.

A: And the mirror at the back reflects the window itself, with two figures in the doorway.
    highlight art:mirror_glass effect detail

    camera reset true

B: Fine, but how do you paint light like that? With what?
    highlight art:gown strength soft lift true

A: Most painters, especially in Italy, still used egg tempera, which dries fast, so colour went on in small, separate strokes.
    highlight art:man_face art:woman_face strength soft

B: So no smooth shading, then.
    highlight art:man_face strength soft lift true

    camera on ["art:folds"]

A: Oil paint stays wet much longer, so Van Eyck could blend soft s (@highlight art:folds@)hadows, like the folds of this green gown.

B: So oil is just slower paint?
    point at.ref art:folds gesture glow

A: It's also clearer paint: thin, see-through layers of colour, called glazes, laid one over another.
    highlight art:gown

A: Light goes in through the glazes, bounces off the paler paint beneath, and comes back out coloured.
    focus art:gown strength strong rest grey

B: So the green isn't just lit from outside. It glows from inside the paint.
    highlight art:folds lift true

    camera reset true

A: A story told a century later says Van Eyck invented oil paint. He didn't, but he showed what it could do.
    highlight art:gown strength soft lift true

A: So this room has two lights: a real window on the left,
    highlight art:window strength strong lift true

    camera on ["art:folds"]

A: and the paint itself.
    highlight art:gown rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

    quiz "A painter wants a velvet sleeve to look deep and glowing. Which follows Van Eyck?" voice a
        right: Thin coats over a lighter layer: the light sinks into the colour, picks it up, and climbs back out, just as in the green gown.
        wrong: Did Van Eyck's glow sit on the surface, or come up from underneath?
        * Mix the colour with lots of white
        + Thin coloured coats over a lighter underlayer
        * One thick coat of the brightest colour
        * A gold outline around the sleeve

B: So that's answer one to our question: paint you can see into, with the light underneath.
    highlight art:gown art:window strength soft lift true

